PROJECT GOAL: We aim to verify if the user needs to set up a build environment to resolve the issue:

1. Does not need build environment
   (The solutions/answers can be validated without build environment, e.g., it's a conceptual issue, documentation issue, or can be verified with simple code analysis)

2. Can be dockerized without any issue
   (The solutions must require a build environment to be verified)

3. Requires build environment but hard to be dockerized or cannot be reproduced now
   (The solution must require a build environment, but it is difficult to containerize for reasons such as: hardware dependencies, network dependencies, external API access with authentication, timing-dependent behaviors, requiring MacOS, multiple users interacting simultaneously, licensed software, etc.)

IMPORTANT GUIDELINES:
- Choose category 1 whenever possible. If the LLM can analyze the issue and verify the solution without needing to build or run the code, always select category 1.
- Only classify an issue as requiring a build environment (categories 2 or 3) when it's ABSOLUTELY NECESSARY to actually build and run the code to verify if the solution works.

GitHub Issue:
Post Date: 2019-02-27T14:33:11Z
Current Date: April 4 2025
Title: Is it possible to define a universal default generic type parameter so that a bottom object like `new None()`, can type check any type of None, such as None<number>, None< ()=>any >?
Description: **In short, TypeScripts uses type {} for default generic type parameter, when T is not provided for `new ClassA<T>(...)`** so Applicative data structure would require developers more concious with types, which is a barrier for more advanced data structure.

Is it possible to define an universal default generic type so that a bottom datastructure like `new None()`, can type check any type of None, such as `None<number>`, `None< ()=>any >`?

**Details:**

I was trying to implement an Applicative data structure in TS as the Applicative of Haskell:

```
(Some f) <*> (Some x) = Some (f x)
(None) <*>(Some x) = None
```

First try is to implement functor/applicative  on Option and subclass Some and None.
Below is the type and class definition:


```
export const id = (a: any) => a

export interface Functor<T> {
  fmap(f: (a: T) => any): Functor<any>
}

export interface Applicative<T> extends Functor<T> {
  ffmap(af: Applicative<(a: T) => any>): Applicative<any>
}

export abstract class Option<T> implements Functor<T> {
  abstract _a: T | undefined

  abstract fmap(f: (a: T) => any): Functor<any>

  *[Symbol.iterator]() {
    yield this._a
  }
}

export class Some<T> extends Option<T> {
  _a: T

  constructor(a: T) {
    super()
    this._a = a
  }

  fmap(f: (a: T) => any): Option<any> {
    return new Some(f(this._a))
  }

  ffmap(a: Option<(a: T) => any>): Option<any> {
    if (a instanceof None) return new None()

    const f = a._a

    if (f === undefined || f === null) return new None()
    else return new Some(f!(this._a))
  }
}

export class None<T> extends Option<T> {
  _a: T | undefined
  constructor() {
    super()
    this._a = undefined
  }

  fmap(f: (a: T) => any): Option<any> {
    return new None()
  }

  ffmap(a: Option<(a: T) => any>): Option<any> {
    return new None()
  }
}

```
I have written some test cases and most work. However when test the case :

```
  test(' Some.ffmap(None) === None', () => {
    const a = new Some(33)

    const af = new None()

    expect(a.ffmap(af)).toEqual(new None())
  })
```

Following error is reported:
```
    src/index.test.ts:116:20 - error TS2345: Argument of type 'None<{}>' is not assignable to parameter of type 'Option<(a: number) => any>'.
      Types of property '_a' are incompatible.
        Type '{} | undefined' is not assignable to type '((a: number) => any) | undefined'.
          Type '{}' is not assignable to type '(a: number) => any'.
            Type '{}' provides no match for the signature '(a: number): any'.

    116     expect(a.ffmap(af)).toEqual(new None())
```

Spcifically, TS consider new None() has the type of new None<{}>(), which is no match with applicative parameter, new None< ()=>any>()

When I change the test case to the following, it works:

```
  test(' Some.fmap(None) = None', () => {
    const a = new None<number>()

    const f = (a: number) => a + 2

    expect(a.fmap(f)).toEqual(a)
  })
```


